              "type": "text",
              "text": "Express the following as a product of polynomials with integer coefficients.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q27-30-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x^4+2x^3-13x^2-14x+24$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)(x+4)$",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^4+2x^3-13x^2-14x+24$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1+2-13-14+24\\\\P(1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x-1$ is a factor. Long division gives\n$$P(x)=(x-1)(x^3+3x^2-10x-24).$$\nFor $Q(x)=x^3+3x^2-10x-24$,\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(-2)&=-8+12+20-24\\\\Q(-2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nDivide $Q(x)$ by $x+2$ and factorise the quadratic:\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(x)&=(x+2)(x^2+x-12)\\\\Q(x)&=(x+2)(x-3)(x+4).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$P(x)=(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)(x+4).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q27-30-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$x^4-3x^3-13x^2+27x+36$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x+1)(x-3)(x+3)(x-4)$",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^4-3x^3-13x^2+27x+36$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-1)&=1+3-13-27+36\\\\P(-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x+1$ is a factor. Long division gives\n$$P(x)=(x+1)(x^3-4x^2-9x+36).$$\nFor $Q(x)=x^3-4x^2-9x+36$,\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(3)&=27-36-27+36\\\\Q(3)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nDivide $Q(x)$ by $x-3$ and factorise the quadratic:\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(x)&=(x-3)(x^2-x-12)\\\\Q(x)&=(x-3)(x+3)(x-4).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$P(x)=(x+1)(x-3)(x+3)(x-4).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q27-30-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$x^4-9x^2+4x+12$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x+1)(x-2)^2(x+3)$",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^4-9x^2+4x+12$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-1)&=1-9-4+12\\\\P(-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x+1$ is a factor. Long division gives\n$$P(x)=(x+1)(x^3-x^2-8x+12).$$\nFor $Q(x)=x^3-x^2-8x+12$,\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(2)&=8-4-16+12\\\\Q(2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nDivide $Q(x)$ by $x-2$ and factorise the quadratic:\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(x)&=(x-2)(x^2+x-6)\\\\Q(x)&=(x-2)^2(x+3).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$P(x)=(x+1)(x-2)^2(x+3).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q27-30-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$x^4-6x^3+4x^2+3x+10$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x-2)(x-5)(x^2+x+1)$",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=x^4-6x^3+4x^2+3x+10$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(2)&=16-48+16+6+10\\\\P(2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x-2$ is a factor. Long division gives\n$$P(x)=(x-2)(x^3-4x^2-4x-5).$$\nFor $Q(x)=x^3-4x^2-4x-5$,\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(5)&=125-100-20-5\\\\Q(5)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nLong division by $x-5$ gives\n$$Q(x)=(x-5)(x^2+x+1).$$\nThe quadratic has discriminant $1-4=-3$, so it has no further real linear factors. Therefore,\n$$P(x)=(x-2)(x-5)(x^2+x+1).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      }
    ]
